  "refuter_findings": [
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:2:1",
      "id": "def-elementary-expansion-and-collapse-of-finite-cw-complexes",
      "location": "Definition, special case where the free face is already present",
      "defect": "false-claim",
      "evidence": "The text says that if “ψ(D^{n−1}_+)=\\overline{e^{n−1}}” and the remaining boundary lies in the lower skeleton, then the inclusion is an elementary expansion; it does not require ψ|D^{n−1}_+ to be a characteristic map. For n=2, take \\bar X=[0,1] with one open 1-cell e, map the upper semicircle by t↦sin²(2πt) onto [0,1], and map the lower semicircle constantly to 0. These satisfy the written conditions, but the attached 2-cell gives H₂(Y,\\bar X)≅Z (there is no new 1-cell), so the inclusion is not a homotopy equivalence and cannot be an elementary expansion.",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "e27a4a738410e098a0d38d80f18e888ae9f4f3640c41780f7832ff789133edf9"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:2:2",
      "id": "def-elementary-expansion-and-collapse-of-finite-cw-complexes",
      "location": "Definition, “Equivalently” paragraph",
      "defect": "false-claim",
      "evidence": "The first bullet requires only that φ|Q^{n−1} be a characteristic map. The dependency def-cell-attachment-by-a-characteristic-map defines this as the quotient map from D^n, whose boundary may identify points. For example, let X be a point, attach a loop e¹ at it, then attach e² with one boundary semicircle given by the characteristic map D¹→e¹∪X and the other mapped to X. The first bullet holds, but D¹→\\overline{e¹}=S¹ is not a homeomorphism, contrary to the claimed equivalent condition.",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "e27a4a738410e098a0d38d80f18e888ae9f4f3640c41780f7832ff789133edf9"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:2:3",
      "id": "def-elementary-expansion-and-collapse-of-finite-cw-complexes",
      "location": "Definition, final sentence (one-cell case)",
      "defect": "false-claim",
      "evidence": "The text says the new edge may join the new vertex “to itself.” A loop at the new vertex has both endpoints outside X, whereas the definition requires the remaining boundary value to lie in X. With X a point, adding a disjoint new vertex and loop adds an S¹ component, so the inclusion is not an elementary expansion.",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "e27a4a738410e098a0d38d80f18e888ae9f4f3640c41780f7832ff789133edf9"
    }
  ],
